WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:11.393
- Okay, so thank you very
much for coming today.

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:16.800
And welcome to the
introductory lecture on autism.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.240
In this lecture, we'll focus
particularly on how autism

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:24.670
expresses itself in the
first three years of life.

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:27.170
We're going to talk about core features,

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:29.270
comorbid characteristics.

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:33.030
We'll talk a little bit about
broader autism phenotype

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:36.540
and spend some time trying to understand

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:39.870
what might be the earliest signs of autism

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:44.280
that are present in babies
way before behavioral

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:45.973
symptoms of autism come online.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.330
Autism is a complex
neurodevelopmental disorder.

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:54.900
It affects in a profound way how children

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.060
interact with other people.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:00.330
Also produces a series
of very unusual behaviors

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:03.150
having to do with
rigidities, repetitive motion

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:05.583
and stereotype interest.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:10.680
It's one of the most prevalent
neurodevelopmental disorders

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:14.253
affecting one in 68 children
in general population.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:20.200
Thus far, we really don't
have biological markers

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.810
or medical tests that can be performed

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:24.610
to determine whether the child

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:27.240
does or doesn't have autism.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.870
For that reason, in order
to diagnose the child,

00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:32.960
we have to perform a close analysis

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:36.070
of the child's symptoms and behaviors.

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:40.190
It's a very complex task,
particularly in young children,

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:42.390
because the expression of autism

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:44.473
is tremendously heterogeneous.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:50.640
Some children with autism
have tremendous sensitivity

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.970
around other people
and they are perturbed,

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:56.050
sometimes even by proximity of others.

00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:58.770
Other children are
actually very interested

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:00.820
interacting with other kids,

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:03.300
but they lack basic knowledge

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.690
of how to carry these
kinds of interactions

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:08.250
in a successful manner.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:10.700
Some children with autism
will never develop speech,

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:14.010
others are very talkative or verbose.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:18.280
Some children have tremendous
learning difficulties,

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:22.220
whereas others are very
bright and talented

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:23.680
in many different ways.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:26.220
So the expression of autism sort of varies

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:28.280
along a number of dimensions,

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.980
and we are still probably to identify

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:34.113
other dimensions along
which our children differ.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:40.335
What also complicates diagnostic
differentiations early on

00:02:40.335 --> 00:02:42.100
is that it's a developmental disorder.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:44.350
What is means that the symptoms of autism

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:47.710
are expressing differently depending

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:51.460
on the child's age and cognitive level.

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:54.380
So we can expect quite
different set of symptoms

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:57.670
in an 18-monther than in a five-year-old,

00:02:57.670 --> 00:02:59.340
than in a 16-year-old,

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:01.550
and in someone who's 25.

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:04.510
So anytime we see a child
for a differential diagnosis

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:06.770
early on, we need to be very mindful.

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:09.880
What can we expect from the child,

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:12.098
from the developmental standpoint

00:03:12.098 --> 00:03:14.728
of what kind of symptoms
would be most diagnostic

00:03:14.728 --> 00:03:16.030
at the given age?

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:18.640
What's important for our discussion today

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:21.653
is that autism starts
before the age of three.

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:25.400
And this is the period,
developmental period

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:28.063
we'll be focusing on during this talk.

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:31.870
So what are the core characteristics

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:34.190
of autism in a young baby?

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:35.453
Or in a toddler?

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:38.630
I think the first thing that we all notice

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:41.751
when we talk into the room and the child,

00:03:41.751 --> 00:03:43.815
they have autism, is that they are not

00:03:43.815 --> 00:03:44.970
really paying attention to things

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:46.920
that otherwise are tremendous important

00:03:48.123 --> 00:03:50.074
to children without social disability.

00:03:50.074 --> 00:03:53.580
And these two things are faces and voices.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:55.900
And actually, limited attention to faces

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:59.210
and limited orienting
to voice or one's name

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:01.540
is sort of one of the first things

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:03.550
that parents begin to notice

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:05.663
when they begin to worry about autism.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:09.760
Another thing that's quite diagnostic

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.500
and specific to autism is a limited drive

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:15.200
to share experiences with others.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:18.670
If you ever interacted with
a 12-monther or 14-monther,

00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:20.990
you know they cannot do a lot,

00:04:20.990 --> 00:04:23.520
but they what can do is to pull you

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:25.730
into their world of experiences

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:28.470
by giving you something,
pointing something,

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:31.530
showing you something for no other reasons

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:35.020
just for sheer pleasure of joining

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:37.700
in the same kind of experience.

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:41.510
This kind of set of behaviors
is profoundly lacking

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:42.733
in children with autism.

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:47.180
Most, or all our children,

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:49.540
when we see them for
differential diagnosis

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:52.596
have delays in language,
and this is not specific

00:04:52.596 --> 00:04:54.625
to autism, a lot of children
have delays in language,

00:04:54.625 --> 00:04:57.140
and they are sometimes
consequential, sometimes not.

00:04:57.140 --> 00:04:59.040
What's very specific to autism,

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:02.670
that our kids major
difficulties in communicating

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:05.450
with other children non-verbally.

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:07.740
They are not using their eyes,

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:11.010
they are not using their
facial expression and gestures

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:14.763
to convey meaning to other
people in the absence of speech.

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:19.108
And that affects how they
interact with the world,

00:05:19.108 --> 00:05:20.880
and in fact, affects quite profoundly

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:23.023
what they learn from the world as well.

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:27.130
At this age, we are
beginning to see a emergence

00:05:27.130 --> 00:05:31.260
of speech, but speech
when it comes online,

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:34.480
it's often stereotyped and
not always fully functional.

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:38.410
So children not always
use the words they have

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:41.280
to communicate in an adaptive way.

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:44.560
The speech is often
repetitive, involves naming,

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:47.040
repetitive naming of objects around,

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:50.120
or repeating verbatim question or phrases

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:51.933
by other people.

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:56.340
Already in the second year of life,

00:05:56.340 --> 00:05:59.950
we are beginning to see emergence
of repetitive behaviors,

00:05:59.950 --> 00:06:03.790
restrictive behaviors
and unusual movements.

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:07.470
They're not horribly,
necessarily, pronounced,

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:09.320
and sometimes it's difficult for someone

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:13.340
who's not well trained in this population

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:16.180
to detect them, but we
are beginning to see

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:19.570
seeking or avoiding of
certain sensory stimuli,

00:06:19.570 --> 00:06:22.590
not protecting ears from the sounds

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:25.350
or seeking spinning objects.

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:28.150
Instead of playing with
toys, with a truck,

00:06:28.150 --> 00:06:30.800
the child may turn it
around and start moving,

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:35.470
flipping the wheels, or begin
to show some unusual movement;

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:39.800
it could be just subtle finger
movements or toe walking.

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:42.363
Again, not very pronounced but present.

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:46.150
So when does autism really emerge?

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:49.180
When do we begin to
see behavioral symptoms

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:52.390
of autism that we can actually diagnose?

00:06:52.390 --> 00:06:55.590
This question has been
on our minds for decades,

00:06:55.590 --> 00:06:58.310
and when I say our minds
I mean our researchers

00:06:58.310 --> 00:06:59.573
in the field of autism.

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:03.300
And we ask this question multiple ways

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:05.860
using retrospective parental reports,

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:08.270
and more recently, by following up

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:11.330
babies who are at risk
for developing autism.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:13.977
And it turns out that in
the first year of life,

00:07:13.977 --> 00:07:15.870
it's really hard to see
the kinds of symptoms

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:18.360
that we would expect in a toddler.

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:20.700
So we cannot sit with the
baby, play with the baby

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:23.130
and see, for instance, poor eye contact

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:25.083
or some gaze aversion.

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:30.050
These behaviors are really
not coming online that early.

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:33.630
But by 12 months, we are beginning to see

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:35.800
delays in social communication,

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:38.593
in use of gestures to communicate.

00:07:39.490 --> 00:07:42.610
There might be some
withdrawal from enjoyment

00:07:42.610 --> 00:07:44.293
of social interactions.

00:07:45.566 --> 00:07:47.770
And parents see it and clinicians see it,

00:07:47.770 --> 00:07:50.010
so these behaviors are noticeable.

00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:53.540
The only thing is that they
are not hard with diagnostic.

00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:55.760
The reason is that some
children who have these

00:07:55.760 --> 00:08:00.320
difficulties at 12 months
actually grow out of them.

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:02.570
And some children who have autism

00:08:02.570 --> 00:08:07.280
are actually still okay
around 12 months of age.

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:10.530
So we are beginning to see
emergence of the symptoms,

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:13.630
but they are not yet diagnostic the way

00:08:13.630 --> 00:08:16.344
we define the symptoms or the way,

00:08:16.344 --> 00:08:17.670
the things we are looking at.

00:08:17.670 --> 00:08:21.560
By 18 months, however,
it's only six months later,

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:24.890
we can reliably diagnose about 40 to 50%

00:08:24.890 --> 00:08:26.090
of children with autism.

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:31.943
And that proportion increases
dramatically afterwards.

00:08:33.590 --> 00:08:36.800
So what does autism might
look like in the first,

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:38.851
at 18 months.

00:08:38.851 --> 00:08:40.857
This is a time when we
say, "Well if the child

00:08:40.857 --> 00:08:44.940
"seems to have symptoms, the
child probably has autism."

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:47.940
So in order answer this
question, we did something,

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:49.520
something very interesting.

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:52.770
We pulled data from across several sites

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:54.800
in the US and in Canada,

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:58.820
and we recruited infant siblings
of children with autism.

00:08:58.820 --> 00:09:01.780
As you might have heard,
due to genetic liability,

00:09:01.780 --> 00:09:04.677
these siblings are at enhanced risk

00:09:04.677 --> 00:09:06.130
of developing autism themselves.

00:09:06.130 --> 00:09:08.560
So with 700 children,
we had a lot of power

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:12.479
to actually ask a question,
what are the unique

00:09:12.479 --> 00:09:14.340
combination of features
that are predictive

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:16.960
of later diagnosis of autism amongst

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:19.047
younger siblings of children with autism?

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:23.510
And we use a machine learning algorithm,

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:24.763
something that we call,

00:09:25.969 --> 00:09:29.340
it's called classification
and regression tree approach,

00:09:29.340 --> 00:09:31.613
which allowed, in which we outputted

00:09:33.783 --> 00:09:36.890
ratings of the child's social
and communicative behaviors,

00:09:36.890 --> 00:09:40.650
repetitive interest on 30 different items.

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:44.080
We allowed for all possible
combination of these features

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:45.730
to be enter into the model,

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:49.540
and then we asked the algorithm
to select those features

00:09:49.540 --> 00:09:52.100
that are most predictive of later outcome.

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:54.450
And we found something really interesting.

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:57.390
If you look at this little
tree, this is a little tree,

00:09:57.390 --> 00:10:00.253
and tells us which
symptoms predict autism.

00:10:02.062 --> 00:10:03.450
And what we found is
that there's (murmurs)

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:07.033
couple of groups here on the, your right,

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:11.550
which sort of were put into this category

00:10:11.550 --> 00:10:13.780
because they have unusual eye contact,

00:10:13.780 --> 00:10:16.500
they don't share or engage others

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:20.930
in their world or activities,

00:10:20.930 --> 00:10:24.112
and they also have very
poor communicative gestures.

00:10:24.112 --> 00:10:25.910
So those are group of
children with autism,

00:10:25.910 --> 00:10:28.170
and these are the prototypical symptoms

00:10:28.170 --> 00:10:30.710
that we looking for at 18 months

00:10:31.550 --> 00:10:33.710
if we are to look for autism:

00:10:33.710 --> 00:10:36.600
lack of eye contact, no sharing

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:38.460
and poor communicative gestures.

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:40.040
So that was great.

00:10:40.040 --> 00:10:43.200
On the other side, however,
we also have some kids

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:45.330
with autism, but they were identified

00:10:45.330 --> 00:10:47.140
by a combination of different features.

00:10:47.140 --> 00:10:50.037
These kids had actually
pretty good eye contact,

00:10:50.037 --> 00:10:53.010
but they were not that bad in
a face-to-face interaction.

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:56.540
What they were lacking,
however, is the behaviors

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:02.780
that sort of aim to engage other peoples

00:11:02.780 --> 00:11:05.365
in their activities, and they were showing

00:11:05.365 --> 00:11:06.770
some emergent repetitive behaviors.

00:11:06.770 --> 00:11:09.040
So why do we have, why different features

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:12.140
predict different subgroups
of kids with autism?

00:11:12.140 --> 00:11:15.130
Well when we looked
actually at their verbal

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:18.700
and nonverbal skills here on the bottom,

00:11:18.700 --> 00:11:21.280
we found that the children who were

00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:23.580
showing this atypical
combination of features

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:26.350
were actually much higher
functioning than those

00:11:26.350 --> 00:11:29.240
that were showing a very
prototypical symptoms.

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:30.610
What does it mean?

00:11:30.610 --> 00:11:33.860
This means that our
current diagnostic criteria

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:35.920
may be calibrated toward children

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:39.310
with greater cognitive
and language delays,

00:11:39.310 --> 00:11:42.860
and might be missing who
are higher functioning,

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:46.430
especially at this very, very young age.

00:11:46.430 --> 00:11:48.570
Something to keep in mind as clinicians

00:11:48.570 --> 00:11:52.570
as we are interacting with
children in diagnostic context.

00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:55.420
The implication of the
study is that right now

00:11:55.420 --> 00:11:57.990
diagnostic tools and screening tools

00:11:57.990 --> 00:12:01.400
work on the assumption
that one size fits all.

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:05.050
What we are saying is that we
may need to begin to calibrate

00:12:05.050 --> 00:12:09.070
to increase the precision
of a diagnostic evaluation.

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:11.410
We may need to calibrate the screening

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:14.850
and diagnostic procedures
to specific subphenotypes

00:12:14.850 --> 00:12:16.253
within the spectrum.

00:12:18.030 --> 00:12:20.440
One of the most things
that we were concerned

00:12:20.440 --> 00:12:24.050
when we started diagnosing
children in such early ages

00:12:24.050 --> 00:12:27.430
is what is the stability of
the diagnosis at this age.

00:12:27.430 --> 00:12:31.930
And what we found, and this
is research here at Yale,

00:12:31.930 --> 00:12:34.720
in England, other colleagues in the US,

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:36.970
that follow children
for one year, two years,

00:12:37.889 --> 00:12:38.800
five years, 15 years,

00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:42.220
is that the stability of
early diagnosis is quite high.

00:12:42.220 --> 00:12:45.720
80 to 90% of children
retained the diagnosis,

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:50.000
and those who don't often have
some residual difficulties

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:53.313
or some other related problems.

00:12:55.020 --> 00:12:58.720
Predictors of later outcome
when we see babies early on

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:01.070
are pretty straightforward.

00:13:01.070 --> 00:13:04.630
Less severe autism symptoms,
higher cognitive skills

00:13:04.630 --> 00:13:07.150
which enable children to learn faster

00:13:07.150 --> 00:13:09.673
and greater array of progress over time.

00:13:11.947 --> 00:13:14.640
What also helps children
in terms of overall outcome

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:17.640
is not to have comorbid disorders,

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:19.990
and I'm going to talk
about this in one second.

00:13:22.340 --> 00:13:26.260
So I mentioned at some point a moment ago,

00:13:26.260 --> 00:13:29.736
I mentioned the special
population of children

00:13:29.736 --> 00:13:31.770
within general population which are

00:13:31.770 --> 00:13:34.550
younger siblings of children with autism.

00:13:34.550 --> 00:13:39.000
Prevalence of autism is
about one in 68 children,

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:40.986
in general population.

00:13:40.986 --> 00:13:43.340
There are certain
subgroups of our population

00:13:43.340 --> 00:13:45.790
where the prevalence of
autism is much higher.

00:13:45.790 --> 00:13:47.720
And it's very important
that, as clinicians,

00:13:47.720 --> 00:13:50.300
we are aware of that
because that might sort

00:13:50.300 --> 00:13:53.410
of put a radar on any time we see a child

00:13:53.410 --> 00:13:56.520
with a specific syndrome or risk factors,

00:13:56.520 --> 00:13:59.914
that they might be,
that we need to monitor

00:13:59.914 --> 00:14:01.090
these children also for autism.

00:14:01.090 --> 00:14:04.170
So one of the groups
that's at the greatest risk

00:14:04.170 --> 00:14:06.680
for developing autism are younger siblings

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:08.540
of kids with autism.

00:14:08.540 --> 00:14:10.530
The reason is genetics.

00:14:10.530 --> 00:14:14.010
And as you might be
aware, genetics factors,

00:14:14.010 --> 00:14:17.890
are extremely powerful as
etiologic factors in autism

00:14:17.890 --> 00:14:22.270
with concordance rates for identical twins

00:14:22.270 --> 00:14:24.363
being around 80% or so.

00:14:25.360 --> 00:14:29.840
So for siblings who are
not identical genetically,

00:14:29.840 --> 00:14:32.740
the concordance rates are somewhat lower.

00:14:32.740 --> 00:14:34.600
And depending on the estimates,

00:14:34.600 --> 00:14:38.840
they differ between one
in 10 or one in five.

00:14:38.840 --> 00:14:41.700
So one in 10 or one in
five of younger siblings

00:14:41.700 --> 00:14:44.227
of children with autism are likely

00:14:44.227 --> 00:14:45.770
to develop the disorder themselves.

00:14:45.770 --> 00:14:49.490
Now this number is moderated
by several other factors.

00:14:49.490 --> 00:14:52.690
One of the factor is how many children

00:14:52.690 --> 00:14:55.043
with autism there are
already in the family.

00:14:56.290 --> 00:14:58.180
If there are two children with autism

00:14:58.180 --> 00:15:00.950
already in the family, the risk doubles.

00:15:00.950 --> 00:15:03.250
The risk for a younger
child to develop autism

00:15:03.250 --> 00:15:05.723
goes up to 30, 40%.

00:15:06.650 --> 00:15:10.320
Another very important factor is the sex

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:12.480
or gender of the younger sibling.

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:15.470
And you can actually see
it here in this graph,

00:15:15.470 --> 00:15:17.510
where we have a comparison
of recurrence rates

00:15:17.510 --> 00:15:20.500
amongst boys and girls as younger sibs.

00:15:20.500 --> 00:15:22.960
And you can see the boys
are much more likely

00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:24.563
to develop autism than girls.

00:15:25.724 --> 00:15:27.853
The risk factor goes up about twice.

00:15:30.130 --> 00:15:33.270
It's twice as high in
boys than it is in girl.

00:15:33.270 --> 00:15:35.930
There's one more factor
that's really fascinating,

00:15:35.930 --> 00:15:38.472
and there's a lot of
work going on right now

00:15:38.472 --> 00:15:40.780
trying to explain this, is this gender,

00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:42.863
sex of the older sibling.

00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:49.230
So if the older sibling, and
these are the bars right here.

00:15:49.230 --> 00:15:53.620
If the older sibling
is a girl versus a boy,

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:56.740
then the likelihood of the next child

00:15:56.740 --> 00:15:58.823
having autism is much higher.

00:16:00.010 --> 00:16:03.060
And it has to do with,
we think it has to do

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:06.060
with the genetic load
that girls need to carry

00:16:06.060 --> 00:16:08.973
in order to reach
affectation status in autism.

00:16:10.350 --> 00:16:14.260
So this is all new emerging science,

00:16:14.260 --> 00:16:16.450
and you probably will be
hearing more about that.

00:16:16.450 --> 00:16:20.263
Important to think about it
when speaking with the parents.

00:16:21.270 --> 00:16:25.530
Now the siblings, as you often hear,

00:16:25.530 --> 00:16:28.090
we're dealing with a
spectrum, autism spectrum,

00:16:28.090 --> 00:16:31.941
which means at one end of
the spectrum we have autism,

00:16:31.941 --> 00:16:34.710
and the other end of the
spectrum we have no autism,

00:16:34.710 --> 00:16:36.270
so to speak, but there's
everything in between.

00:16:36.270 --> 00:16:40.220
So when you look at this
figure here on the right,

00:16:40.220 --> 00:16:42.750
you can see that, sort of metaphorically,

00:16:42.750 --> 00:16:46.140
that if we think that
there are children here

00:16:48.600 --> 00:16:51.620
who have autism, younger
siblings who have autism,

00:16:51.620 --> 00:16:54.163
and then children who
are developing typically,

00:16:55.190 --> 00:16:57.150
there is a group in the middle,

00:16:57.150 --> 00:17:00.500
and this is the group we call
broader autism phenotype.

00:17:00.500 --> 00:17:02.190
These are the children who are at risk

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:04.850
for developing some social difficulties,

00:17:04.850 --> 00:17:08.060
not necessarily meeting
criteria for autism.

00:17:08.060 --> 00:17:12.450
And they're also at risk for
developing other difficulties.

00:17:12.450 --> 00:17:16.520
Already when they are as young
as 18 months or 24 months,

00:17:16.520 --> 00:17:18.730
we see that there is a
significant proportion

00:17:18.730 --> 00:17:20.790
of younger siblings of
children with autism

00:17:20.790 --> 00:17:23.990
who have language delays,
social difficulties,

00:17:23.990 --> 00:17:27.453
emotional regulation
difficulties, motor difficulties.

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:31.110
And what's very important to keep in mind

00:17:31.110 --> 00:17:33.363
that when these children grow older,

00:17:34.640 --> 00:17:37.120
during school age and adolescence,

00:17:37.120 --> 00:17:40.180
these children are twice
as likely to develop

00:17:40.180 --> 00:17:43.525
affective disorders, anxiety, ADHD,

00:17:43.525 --> 00:17:45.600
and a host of other difficulties.

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:49.210
So again, the reason
why I'm bringing it up

00:17:49.210 --> 00:17:53.100
is that we need to be very
sensitive to clinical needs

00:17:53.100 --> 00:17:55.980
of younger siblings of
children with autism

00:17:55.980 --> 00:17:59.580
who do not develop autism themselves.

00:17:59.580 --> 00:18:01.740
And if there are symptoms present,

00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:04.287
we should be actually acting on them,

00:18:04.287 --> 00:18:07.440
not worrying is it autism, it's
not autism, it's irrelevant.

00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:10.370
If the child is facing difficulties

00:18:10.370 --> 00:18:14.123
because of their vulnerabilities,
we need to intervene.

00:18:15.570 --> 00:18:19.690
It might, in fact,
prevent some of the later

00:18:19.690 --> 00:18:24.680
mental health disorders that
we see quite so frequently

00:18:24.680 --> 00:18:25.793
in this population.

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:30.240
There are other groups that are also

00:18:30.240 --> 00:18:32.610
at increased risk for autism,
and one of them are preemies.

00:18:32.610 --> 00:18:34.970
And as you can see here on the top,

00:18:34.970 --> 00:18:38.370
the risk for autism increases

00:18:38.370 --> 00:18:40.630
as the gestational age decreases,

00:18:40.630 --> 00:18:44.980
which very high risk for autism in babies

00:18:44.980 --> 00:18:48.850
who are born with gestational
age of 23 or 24 weeks.

00:18:48.850 --> 00:18:50.900
It's about 15% or so.

00:18:50.900 --> 00:18:53.991
Whereas the average for
the entire preemie group

00:18:53.991 --> 00:18:55.223
is 7% or so.

00:18:56.095 --> 00:18:57.600
There's a number of genetic syndromes

00:18:57.600 --> 00:19:00.900
where we see higher incidence of autism,

00:19:00.900 --> 00:19:05.440
including Angelman syndrome,
Phelan McDermid syndrome,

00:19:05.440 --> 00:19:08.910
and more recently,
congenital heart disease.

00:19:08.910 --> 00:19:11.350
That group is still under investigation.

00:19:11.350 --> 00:19:14.540
We don't have a lot of really good studies

00:19:14.540 --> 00:19:17.290
evaluating prevalence
of autism in this group,

00:19:17.290 --> 00:19:21.050
but current estimates sort of vary

00:19:21.050 --> 00:19:24.620
between maybe three, maybe 5% or so.

00:19:24.620 --> 00:19:27.270
I'm sure we'll hear more
about it in the near future.

00:19:29.870 --> 00:19:32.780
Six differences in autism, right?

00:19:32.780 --> 00:19:35.660
Like many other developmental disorders,

00:19:35.660 --> 00:19:38.363
boys are more likely to develop autism.

00:19:39.300 --> 00:19:43.790
If you read DSM-5, the ratio
quoted there is four to one,

00:19:43.790 --> 00:19:45.630
four boys, one girl.

00:19:45.630 --> 00:19:47.900
If you look at more recent studies

00:19:47.900 --> 00:19:50.480
that actually are based on population,

00:19:50.480 --> 00:19:54.420
they are not as, there may be less bias

00:19:54.420 --> 00:19:57.790
or more inclusive; the ratio
actually goes a little higher,

00:19:57.790 --> 00:20:02.043
goes down, and the recent
estimates are just three to one.

00:20:03.952 --> 00:20:06.922
It's a very interesting
question, why, right?

00:20:06.922 --> 00:20:09.110
Why more boys, why fewer girls?

00:20:09.110 --> 00:20:11.640
When we look at the
severity of autism symptoms

00:20:11.640 --> 00:20:15.423
between boys and girls, in
terms of social difficulties,

00:20:16.972 --> 00:20:19.400
the severity's quite
comparable between the groups.

00:20:19.400 --> 00:20:22.280
The girls, however, present
themselves somewhat differently

00:20:22.280 --> 00:20:24.500
because they have typically fewer

00:20:24.500 --> 00:20:27.250
or not as intense repetitive behaviors,

00:20:27.250 --> 00:20:30.770
and also they tend to be more introverted

00:20:30.770 --> 00:20:33.830
or have more anxiety,
have more depression,

00:20:33.830 --> 00:20:35.900
so they tend to be a little bit quieter.

00:20:35.900 --> 00:20:38.230
Boys, on the other hand, are very visible,

00:20:38.230 --> 00:20:41.400
a lot of sir-to-pees, and
they also very rambunctious,

00:20:41.400 --> 00:20:44.790
very active and prone to
externalizing disorders,

00:20:44.790 --> 00:20:47.006
including behavioral problems,

00:20:47.006 --> 00:20:49.140
so they are very, very, very visible.

00:20:49.140 --> 00:20:53.180
So in our field, again,
this is one (murmurs) areas

00:20:53.180 --> 00:20:55.970
to watch in the coming years.

00:20:55.970 --> 00:20:57.790
So we've been asking the question,

00:20:57.790 --> 00:21:02.140
what produces this disparity
between males and females?

00:21:02.140 --> 00:21:04.740
One set of answers have to do with how

00:21:04.740 --> 00:21:06.980
we diagnose the kids.

00:21:06.980 --> 00:21:10.610
Is the current diagnosis sort
of a social construction,

00:21:10.610 --> 00:21:15.610
or is the disparity due
to biological factors?

00:21:16.130 --> 00:21:19.147
These two hypotheses, or
these two groups of hypotheses

00:21:19.147 --> 00:21:22.980
are not mutually exclusive,
they are probably both,

00:21:22.980 --> 00:21:25.270
most certainly both at play.

00:21:25.270 --> 00:21:27.690
So let me tell you about the first one.

00:21:27.690 --> 00:21:32.083
So why do we have fewer girls than boys?

00:21:33.330 --> 00:21:36.120
Very interesting, several studies

00:21:36.120 --> 00:21:38.500
evaluated severity of autism symptoms

00:21:38.500 --> 00:21:43.430
in large population of children,
in England, in the States.

00:21:43.430 --> 00:21:46.950
And they found that boys and girls

00:21:46.950 --> 00:21:50.250
may have the same severity
of autism symptoms,

00:21:50.250 --> 00:21:53.110
but boys are more likely to receive

00:21:53.110 --> 00:21:55.593
a diagnosis of autism in their community.

00:21:57.240 --> 00:22:01.700
So there's something, some
kind of a diagnostic bias

00:22:02.617 --> 00:22:07.617
that prevents girls who are
clearly socially vulnerable

00:22:08.270 --> 00:22:09.940
from receiving the diagnosis

00:22:09.940 --> 00:22:12.800
and being treated appropriately.

00:22:12.800 --> 00:22:15.830
And the idea is that perhaps
the diagnostic criteria

00:22:15.830 --> 00:22:19.350
that we are operating within

00:22:19.350 --> 00:22:22.550
have been created based on
how autism expresses in boys,

00:22:22.550 --> 00:22:24.080
not how it expresses in girls.

00:22:24.080 --> 00:22:27.030
So for that reason, it
might not just select those

00:22:27.030 --> 00:22:29.363
who fit that description.

00:22:30.970 --> 00:22:34.560
It's a little circular sort of way

00:22:34.560 --> 00:22:37.713
of designing the diagnostic
criteria and using them.

00:22:38.650 --> 00:22:41.610
And this area, again,
is under investigation.

00:22:41.610 --> 00:22:44.793
We'll hear more about it
in the very near future.

00:22:45.640 --> 00:22:48.350
Another biological factors that have been

00:22:48.350 --> 00:22:51.090
proposed to account for this disparity

00:22:51.090 --> 00:22:55.170
are heterogeneity, for
instance, in terms of etiology,

00:22:55.170 --> 00:22:57.658
and that there might be different factors

00:22:57.658 --> 00:22:59.282
on both genetic and environmental

00:22:59.282 --> 00:23:01.620
that give rise in males and females.

00:23:01.620 --> 00:23:05.910
The hypothesis that has
been more strongly supported

00:23:05.910 --> 00:23:08.510
recently is the hypothesis which suggests

00:23:08.510 --> 00:23:10.400
that the ratio is due to the fact

00:23:10.400 --> 00:23:13.680
that the girls may have some
kind of protective factors

00:23:13.680 --> 00:23:18.118
which are preventing them from expressing

00:23:18.118 --> 00:23:22.600
symptoms of autism unless they
receive a major genetic hit.

00:23:22.600 --> 00:23:25.420
So in other words, they need to have more

00:23:26.330 --> 00:23:28.860
atypicalities in terms of genetics,

00:23:28.860 --> 00:23:30.930
genetic or epigenetic factors in order

00:23:30.930 --> 00:23:33.340
to reach affectation status.

00:23:33.340 --> 00:23:36.370
We don't know exactly what this
protective factors might be,

00:23:36.370 --> 00:23:37.910
there are several hypotheses;

00:23:37.910 --> 00:23:39.520
one suggesting thought there might be

00:23:39.520 --> 00:23:41.890
some genetic X link factors

00:23:41.890 --> 00:23:43.530
that are protecting the girls.

00:23:43.530 --> 00:23:46.210
Or there might be some hormonal factors

00:23:46.210 --> 00:23:48.560
that also are sort of lessening

00:23:48.560 --> 00:23:53.560
some of the edges or around
the social difficulties.

00:23:56.210 --> 00:23:57.780
Speaking of protective factors,

00:23:57.780 --> 00:24:02.422
we did a study not long ago where actually

00:24:02.422 --> 00:24:05.100
describably one of the
first, or if not the first,

00:24:05.100 --> 00:24:08.060
sort of evidence for
potential protective factor

00:24:08.060 --> 00:24:10.360
already in young babies.

00:24:10.360 --> 00:24:13.430
This is a study in which we
use eye-tracking technology

00:24:13.430 --> 00:24:17.350
to evaluate how babies allocate attention

00:24:17.350 --> 00:24:22.340
in response to a person who
is interacting with them,

00:24:22.340 --> 00:24:25.970
speaking to them, making eye contact.

00:24:25.970 --> 00:24:28.740
There's some toys, you can see the example

00:24:28.740 --> 00:24:31.380
of a screenshot from that video.

00:24:31.380 --> 00:24:33.350
So at any given point,
the child needs to make

00:24:33.350 --> 00:24:35.990
a decision where to move their eyes.

00:24:35.990 --> 00:24:40.420
And we conducted a study in
six, nine and 12 month-old

00:24:40.420 --> 00:24:43.100
younger siblings of children with autism.

00:24:43.100 --> 00:24:46.050
And then we compare how
high risk and low risk,

00:24:46.050 --> 00:24:49.250
these are the children without
an older sibling with autism,

00:24:49.250 --> 00:24:50.920
perform on this task.

00:24:50.920 --> 00:24:52.920
In the red, you see girls;

00:24:52.920 --> 00:24:57.120
solid line, girls who are
at high risk for autism.

00:24:57.120 --> 00:24:58.570
In black, you see boys;

00:24:58.570 --> 00:25:02.690
solid line, boys who
are at risk for autism.

00:25:02.690 --> 00:25:05.777
And as you can see, something
is really interesting.

00:25:05.777 --> 00:25:09.270
This red line (murmurs)
is really, really high,

00:25:09.270 --> 00:25:12.170
both in terms of overall
attention to the task

00:25:12.170 --> 00:25:14.980
or to the social scene and also in terms

00:25:14.980 --> 00:25:18.033
of attention to the face of the speaker.

00:25:18.930 --> 00:25:23.030
It's a very interesting,
intriguing and puzzling finding.

00:25:23.030 --> 00:25:25.770
It might suggest that younger sister

00:25:25.770 --> 00:25:28.470
of children with autism, very early on

00:25:28.470 --> 00:25:31.550
are employing perhaps a
compensatory strategies

00:25:31.550 --> 00:25:35.570
to try to get some more
out of social interactions,

00:25:35.570 --> 00:25:37.785
trying to maybe remedy some

00:25:37.785 --> 00:25:39.493
of the vulnerabilities they have.

00:25:40.630 --> 00:25:42.950
And we are following up these studies,

00:25:42.950 --> 00:25:45.240
and we might have an
answer what it actually is

00:25:45.240 --> 00:25:47.920
and what it does, but this
is a first illustration

00:25:47.920 --> 00:25:51.633
of a potential, a protective
factor already in infancy.

00:25:54.290 --> 00:25:57.520
So just to sum up our
clinical implications

00:25:58.930 --> 00:26:01.703
of the discussions we just had:

00:26:02.600 --> 00:26:06.110
early diagnosis is stable, we have tools

00:26:06.110 --> 00:26:09.270
to diagnose children in a reliable way.

00:26:09.270 --> 00:26:11.130
We need to be very careful how we do it

00:26:11.130 --> 00:26:13.340
because we need to
consider multiple factors,

00:26:13.340 --> 00:26:16.980
not just symptoms of autism,
but also the child's language,

00:26:16.980 --> 00:26:20.840
cognition, family
factors, medical factors.

00:26:20.840 --> 00:26:22.810
But when we take it under consideration

00:26:22.810 --> 00:26:27.130
and the child's presentation
is consistent with autism,

00:26:27.130 --> 00:26:30.180
that diagnosis is relatively stable.

00:26:30.180 --> 00:26:32.530
There's no reason to wait for anything.

00:26:32.530 --> 00:26:35.680
There's a reason to act and help families

00:26:35.680 --> 00:26:40.680
move toward implementing
intervention services.

00:26:41.560 --> 00:26:44.620
Important to keep in mind
that not all toddlers

00:26:44.620 --> 00:26:47.110
are going to be very prototypical.

00:26:47.110 --> 00:26:48.940
The girls might look a little different,

00:26:48.940 --> 00:26:51.220
they might look better,
they might be camouflaging

00:26:51.220 --> 00:26:52.663
some of their difficulties.

00:26:54.160 --> 00:26:56.760
Also, kids who are higher functioning

00:26:56.760 --> 00:26:59.920
might be not quite showing
the prototypical symptoms

00:26:59.920 --> 00:27:01.503
that we expect at this age.

00:27:02.340 --> 00:27:05.477
Given the high risk that we know,

00:27:05.477 --> 00:27:07.700
it's number of populations,
or subgroups within

00:27:07.700 --> 00:27:11.620
our population, has
enhanced risk for autism.

00:27:11.620 --> 00:27:14.810
We have a mandate to actually
monitor these children

00:27:14.810 --> 00:27:18.190
and screen them to make
sure that we detect

00:27:18.190 --> 00:27:21.083
social difficulties as soon as possible.

00:27:22.440 --> 00:27:25.490
And in terms of predictors of outcome,

00:27:25.490 --> 00:27:29.480
we don't have tremendously
specific measures,

00:27:29.480 --> 00:27:32.620
but just on a very general level,

00:27:32.620 --> 00:27:35.410
having better cognitive
skills, verbal skills

00:27:35.410 --> 00:27:38.110
and lesser severity,
better progress or response

00:27:38.974 --> 00:27:40.950
to treatment within the first month

00:27:40.950 --> 00:27:42.950
typically bode well with later outcomes.

00:27:49.220 --> 00:27:54.174
All right, so we covered
core symptoms of autism,

00:27:54.174 --> 00:27:56.250
and I wish this is where we could end.

00:27:56.250 --> 00:27:58.890
I wish this was all the problems

00:27:58.890 --> 00:28:02.150
children with autism
and their families had.

00:28:02.150 --> 00:28:03.993
It's not true, unfortunately.

00:28:04.950 --> 00:28:09.610
The problem is that 70%
of children with autism

00:28:09.610 --> 00:28:12.883
has at least one comorbid condition.

00:28:13.860 --> 00:28:16.198
And when I say comorbid condition,

00:28:16.198 --> 00:28:20.670
I mean a diagnosis of
ADHD, affective disorder,

00:28:20.670 --> 00:28:24.410
anxiety, schizophrenia,

00:28:24.410 --> 00:28:26.350
many, many, many other disorders.

00:28:26.350 --> 00:28:29.783
Their prevalence is higher
than in general population.

00:28:31.430 --> 00:28:34.540
So in some children, or a lot of children

00:28:34.540 --> 00:28:37.440
actually have more than one
comorbid disorder occurring.

00:28:37.440 --> 00:28:39.360
What it tells us is that the factors

00:28:39.360 --> 00:28:41.880
that are driving psychopathology

00:28:43.240 --> 00:28:46.333
are overlapping to a large extent.

00:28:47.460 --> 00:28:51.630
But what happens is that
when a child with autism

00:28:51.630 --> 00:28:54.610
has a comorbid disorder on top of it,

00:28:54.610 --> 00:28:59.360
it always, it never bodes well
for the long-term outcomes.

00:28:59.360 --> 00:29:02.320
These children struggle more,
they have more difficulties

00:29:02.320 --> 00:29:05.610
in adaption, social functioning.

00:29:05.610 --> 00:29:08.210
They are less likely to get more,

00:29:08.210 --> 00:29:13.210
they may have trouble getting
an educational placement

00:29:13.470 --> 00:29:17.110
that's better matched
with their overall skills.

00:29:17.110 --> 00:29:19.210
There's a higher risk of hospitalization,

00:29:19.210 --> 00:29:21.713
suicidality and many other problems.

00:29:22.640 --> 00:29:25.210
For many years, these kind of problems

00:29:25.210 --> 00:29:27.587
are overshadowed by autism.

00:29:27.587 --> 00:29:30.230
And we typically did not
diagnose comorbid disorders

00:29:30.230 --> 00:29:32.603
in children with autism, it was like,

00:29:32.603 --> 00:29:33.827
"Oh, you know, he has
autism so he's active.

00:29:33.827 --> 00:29:36.857
"Oh, you know, he's moody
because he has autism."

00:29:37.880 --> 00:29:41.880
DSM-5 changed that, and right now a child

00:29:41.880 --> 00:29:45.143
with autism can carry
co-occurring conditions as well.

00:29:47.490 --> 00:29:50.960
What's important is that,
for us as a clinician,

00:29:50.960 --> 00:29:53.390
to also keep in mind
that we are not horribly

00:29:53.390 --> 00:29:56.940
well equipped to differentiate or diagnose

00:29:56.940 --> 00:29:58.660
these disorders in kids with autism

00:29:58.660 --> 00:30:01.780
because there's so much mooshing around

00:30:01.780 --> 00:30:03.460
of various symptoms

00:30:07.360 --> 00:30:09.080
and their manifestations.

00:30:09.080 --> 00:30:11.550
There's a lot of really, really good work

00:30:11.550 --> 00:30:13.880
happening right now in our field

00:30:13.880 --> 00:30:16.630
to create better screening instruments

00:30:16.630 --> 00:30:20.510
for anxiety, for instance, or
ADHD for children with autism,

00:30:20.510 --> 00:30:24.620
and also to create treatments
that would be effective

00:30:24.620 --> 00:30:27.430
in children with autism as much as they're

00:30:27.430 --> 00:30:30.360
effective in general population.

00:30:30.360 --> 00:30:32.860
So again, something to think about,

00:30:32.860 --> 00:30:37.623
look for, and more information is coming.

00:30:41.200 --> 00:30:44.020
Why is it important when
we talk about toddlers?

00:30:44.020 --> 00:30:46.150
Because we are focusing
on kids under three,

00:30:46.150 --> 00:30:49.740
we typically don't diagnose anxiety, ADHD,

00:30:49.740 --> 00:30:52.260
affective disorders in kids under three,

00:30:52.260 --> 00:30:54.650
so why am I talking about it?

00:30:54.650 --> 00:30:57.650
Well I am talking about it because we know

00:30:57.650 --> 00:31:01.400
from extensive research
in general population

00:31:02.370 --> 00:31:04.570
that there are certain precursors

00:31:04.570 --> 00:31:07.190
or signs of upcoming difficulties,

00:31:07.190 --> 00:31:10.993
internalizing, externalizing
problems, already in infancy.

00:31:11.970 --> 00:31:14.480
And they are conceptualized
under the umbrella

00:31:14.480 --> 00:31:17.410
of temperament, which is
sort of a biological tendency

00:31:17.410 --> 00:31:21.455
to respond in certain ways with regard

00:31:21.455 --> 00:31:23.160
to positive affectivity, joy,

00:31:23.160 --> 00:31:26.240
or negative affectivity
such as fear and anger,

00:31:26.240 --> 00:31:29.690
and also ability to regulate emotions

00:31:29.690 --> 00:31:34.343
that arise in response to
internal or external challenges.

00:31:35.230 --> 00:31:37.747
What's interesting that these patterns

00:31:37.747 --> 00:31:39.602
can be detected as early as six months,

00:31:39.602 --> 00:31:42.150
and they are relatively stable over time.

00:31:42.150 --> 00:31:46.820
And there might be shaped
a little bit by experience,

00:31:46.820 --> 00:31:49.670
but they are relatively stable over time.

00:31:49.670 --> 00:31:51.860
Extensive research in general population

00:31:51.860 --> 00:31:55.160
suggests that a typical positive
and negative affectivity

00:31:56.620 --> 00:31:59.080
are related to later internalizing

00:31:59.080 --> 00:32:01.200
and externalizing problems.

00:32:01.200 --> 00:32:04.380
So for us, paying attention
to how our toddlers are doing

00:32:04.380 --> 00:32:09.380
in terms of their regulation
and emotional expressions

00:32:09.690 --> 00:32:12.710
in response to real-world challenges is,

00:32:12.710 --> 00:32:16.430
it makes a lot of sense, and
I'll tell you why right now.

00:32:16.430 --> 00:32:21.430
So we evaluated
temperamental characteristics

00:32:21.430 --> 00:32:23.975
of toddlers with autism.

00:32:23.975 --> 00:32:26.050
This was probably one
of the largest studies

00:32:28.872 --> 00:32:32.200
of temperament in young
children with autism.

00:32:32.200 --> 00:32:34.410
The strength of the study
was not only its size,

00:32:34.410 --> 00:32:37.520
but we also compared children with autism

00:32:37.520 --> 00:32:40.447
with children with other
developmental delays.

00:32:40.447 --> 00:32:43.130
It's not a big art to compare
autism with typically kids.

00:32:43.130 --> 00:32:45.720
What we want to see how
children with autism

00:32:45.720 --> 00:32:48.550
differ from children with
developmental delays.

00:32:48.550 --> 00:32:53.540
And what we found was
that children with autism

00:32:53.540 --> 00:32:57.680
seemed to display less
intensely positive emotion,

00:32:57.680 --> 00:33:00.580
they seem to experience
less positive emotion

00:33:00.580 --> 00:33:05.580
in response to normative
real-world environment.

00:33:07.180 --> 00:33:11.720
They also seem to be more
negatively biased early on,

00:33:11.720 --> 00:33:15.600
and they have difficulties
regulating emotions that arise

00:33:16.717 --> 00:33:19.690
when they are challenged by new things

00:33:19.690 --> 00:33:22.340
or things they don't necessarily like.

00:33:22.340 --> 00:33:26.280
What was very, very interesting
is that, at this age,

00:33:26.280 --> 00:33:28.580
severity of these emotional difficulties

00:33:28.580 --> 00:33:31.180
was not really associated
with severity of autism,

00:33:31.180 --> 00:33:34.210
which suggests that there may
be totally different domains

00:33:34.210 --> 00:33:38.590
of functioning that might arise
maybe from different sources

00:33:38.590 --> 00:33:43.440
and then interact to produce
complex phenotypes later on.

00:33:43.440 --> 00:33:46.580
We follow this group of
children when they were four,

00:33:46.580 --> 00:33:48.420
and there was very high stability.

00:33:48.420 --> 00:33:50.760
You can see these correlations here

00:33:50.760 --> 00:33:54.480
in the 50s and 60s, very high
stability within our group

00:33:54.480 --> 00:33:56.950
in terms of what were they

00:33:56.950 --> 00:34:00.970
temperamental vulnerabilities early on.

00:34:00.970 --> 00:34:03.390
What was also very important is that

00:34:03.390 --> 00:34:05.330
this early emotional vulnerabilities

00:34:05.330 --> 00:34:09.580
contributed later to
severity of autism symptoms

00:34:09.580 --> 00:34:12.090
as measure one or two years later.

00:34:12.090 --> 00:34:15.350
So it's an interesting example,
have two different domains

00:34:15.350 --> 00:34:18.973
might interact and produce complex,

00:34:20.057 --> 00:34:23.566
reinforce each other and
sort of produce complex

00:34:23.566 --> 00:34:25.470
phenotypes in the autism group.

00:34:25.470 --> 00:34:30.303
So just to sum up, we have,

00:34:32.790 --> 00:34:35.070
there are very good
reasons why we should be

00:34:35.070 --> 00:34:38.130
monitoring emotional development
and motion regulation

00:34:38.130 --> 00:34:40.050
in children with autism.

00:34:40.050 --> 00:34:42.840
It's not everything that
you see is part of autism.

00:34:42.840 --> 00:34:46.370
It might be part of a
different set of risk factors

00:34:46.370 --> 00:34:50.200
that are likely to
produce symptoms later on

00:34:50.200 --> 00:34:52.903
that will make their
lives of kids with autism

00:34:54.360 --> 00:34:57.520
less adaptive, and lives of their families

00:34:57.520 --> 00:34:59.133
a little bit more complicated.

00:35:02.170 --> 00:35:05.892
Because of the fact that
the various comorbidities

00:35:05.892 --> 00:35:08.220
come online at different ages,

00:35:08.220 --> 00:35:11.220
we might start with some shyness early on,

00:35:11.220 --> 00:35:16.010
ADHD, anxiety, then we get
into affective disorders,

00:35:16.010 --> 00:35:17.600
and so forth and so forth.

00:35:17.600 --> 00:35:21.060
There's a need of
monitoring kids with autism

00:35:21.060 --> 00:35:23.600
continuously for these problems.

00:35:23.600 --> 00:35:26.201
It's not screening them
at 18 months and say,

00:35:26.201 --> 00:35:27.034
"Uh, it's fine."

00:35:27.034 --> 00:35:30.330
No, because there are problem
that will arise overtime,

00:35:30.330 --> 00:35:33.820
so screening kids for these
emotional difficulties

00:35:33.820 --> 00:35:36.980
throughout their early
childhood and early school age

00:35:36.980 --> 00:35:39.970
is clinically indicated.

00:35:39.970 --> 00:35:44.970
And we hope that adequate
or precise detection

00:35:45.080 --> 00:35:48.150
of these problems and intervention

00:35:48.150 --> 00:35:52.563
will help improve long-term
outcomes in kids with autism.

00:35:58.440 --> 00:36:00.440
All right, we're gonna switch gears now.

00:36:01.952 --> 00:36:05.430
I'm taking out my clinician hat,

00:36:05.430 --> 00:36:07.823
and I'm putting on my researcher hat,

00:36:09.030 --> 00:36:13.370
because we're going to talk
about the developmental period

00:36:13.370 --> 00:36:17.850
that sort of spans between
birth and about 12 months or so.

00:36:17.850 --> 00:36:22.850
And there are very few
symptoms that we can call

00:36:23.960 --> 00:36:26.730
symptoms of autism during this time.

00:36:26.730 --> 00:36:30.170
So we are studying this
time in development

00:36:30.170 --> 00:36:33.970
because we want to understand
what's really happening.

00:36:33.970 --> 00:36:37.940
So is it true that autism
really doesn't start

00:36:37.940 --> 00:36:40.050
until the second year of life?

00:36:40.050 --> 00:36:43.850
Or is it true that there
are pathogenic factors

00:36:43.850 --> 00:36:45.900
which are already unfolding,

00:36:45.900 --> 00:36:48.415
but they are unfolding on a level

00:36:48.415 --> 00:36:51.165
that it's more difficult to
capture with the naked eye?

00:36:54.760 --> 00:36:57.340
One of the line, a line
of research in my lab

00:36:57.340 --> 00:37:00.740
is focus in trying to understand why

00:37:01.620 --> 00:37:05.220
toddlers with autism have
such limited attention,

00:37:05.220 --> 00:37:08.280
of ability to select for processing,

00:37:08.280 --> 00:37:11.533
people's faces, especially
when the people are speaking.

00:37:12.820 --> 00:37:16.180
It's a very important, it's
a core symptom of autism.

00:37:16.180 --> 00:37:19.120
It's very important because
we learn by directing

00:37:19.120 --> 00:37:22.410
our attention toward important
sources of information.

00:37:22.410 --> 00:37:25.620
In other words, attention gates learning.

00:37:25.620 --> 00:37:28.220
If we are not paying attention
to people and their faces,

00:37:28.220 --> 00:37:30.390
we not try to decode what they speak

00:37:30.390 --> 00:37:33.700
while attending to their facial features,

00:37:33.700 --> 00:37:36.610
we're missing a tremendous
amount of information,

00:37:36.610 --> 00:37:39.560
which is gonna affect not only
how they develop language,

00:37:39.560 --> 00:37:42.723
but also how they
develop social expertise.

00:37:43.760 --> 00:37:47.050
And Leo Kanner, this gentleman here,

00:37:47.050 --> 00:37:51.180
in 1943, he already noted that this kind

00:37:51.180 --> 00:37:55.200
of limited attention to
people's faces and voices

00:37:55.200 --> 00:37:58.113
is present in autism very, very early on.

00:38:01.620 --> 00:38:06.600
So what we did is we created
an experimental paradigm

00:38:06.600 --> 00:38:10.290
which allowed us to replicate
what we see in real world,

00:38:10.290 --> 00:38:13.210
in the lab, and that
replication is important

00:38:13.210 --> 00:38:15.080
because then we can take it apart.

00:38:15.080 --> 00:38:18.210
We can manipulate experimentally
what children see,

00:38:18.210 --> 00:38:20.590
what do they hear, and try to understand

00:38:20.590 --> 00:38:24.030
what's generating this profound impairment

00:38:24.030 --> 00:38:26.483
in selective attention to social targets.

00:38:28.106 --> 00:38:30.040
And the study involve a video,

00:38:30.040 --> 00:38:32.410
I mentioned it before, but now I actually

00:38:32.410 --> 00:38:34.290
will give you some more details.

00:38:34.290 --> 00:38:39.170
In this video, this very
wonderful young woman

00:38:39.170 --> 00:38:42.240
is speaking to the camera,
trying to engage the viewer

00:38:42.240 --> 00:38:44.480
using eye contact, speech.

00:38:44.480 --> 00:38:47.680
Occasionally, she turns
toward materials on the table,

00:38:47.680 --> 00:38:51.713
makes a sandwich; occasionally,
the toys start moving.

00:38:52.579 --> 00:38:55.400
So the child has to decide
millisecond by millisecond

00:38:55.400 --> 00:38:59.033
where to direct their precious
attentional resources,

00:39:00.494 --> 00:39:02.540
and where the information
is, what they are going

00:39:02.540 --> 00:39:03.790
to learn from this scene.

00:39:04.710 --> 00:39:09.670
When we take group of
typically developing children

00:39:09.670 --> 00:39:12.620
and show them this video, you can see

00:39:12.620 --> 00:39:16.623
that they are behaving in
a very orchestrated way.

00:39:19.360 --> 00:39:22.360
- [Young Woman] Hi, again,
sweetie, how are you doing?

00:39:22.360 --> 00:39:23.583
Are you having fun?

00:39:24.500 --> 00:39:26.363
You look so cute today.

00:39:28.956 --> 00:39:32.289
(rattling and clanking)

00:39:33.770 --> 00:39:36.980
- So as you can see, all
children kind of converge.

00:39:36.980 --> 00:39:38.503
When she's making a sandwich,

00:39:39.338 --> 00:39:40.580
they observe goal-oriented action.

00:39:40.580 --> 00:39:42.960
When she speaks, they monitor her face.

00:39:42.960 --> 00:39:45.320
When something happens
perceptually salient,

00:39:45.320 --> 00:39:47.000
they look at her face.

00:39:47.000 --> 00:39:51.850
When we repeated the study
with children who have autism,

00:39:51.850 --> 00:39:54.500
and you can see the graphs down in red,

00:39:54.500 --> 00:39:57.290
that this is group of
children with autism;

00:39:57.290 --> 00:39:59.660
gray, developmental delay; and blue,

00:39:59.660 --> 00:40:02.310
these are typically developing children.

00:40:02.310 --> 00:40:04.520
You can see that children with autism

00:40:04.520 --> 00:40:06.120
are not completely lost.

00:40:06.120 --> 00:40:10.340
They are behaving quite
well in many respects.

00:40:10.340 --> 00:40:13.300
However, there's one situation
in which their behavior

00:40:13.300 --> 00:40:15.310
diverges from the other groups.

00:40:15.310 --> 00:40:17.880
This is the situation when
she looks at the camera

00:40:17.880 --> 00:40:20.940
and speaks, trying to engage
the viewer in the sort of,

00:40:20.940 --> 00:40:24.800
using prototypical dyadic
bids for engagement.

00:40:24.800 --> 00:40:27.410
In this situation, they look less at faces

00:40:27.410 --> 00:40:30.160
than children with
comparable cognitive skills

00:40:30.160 --> 00:40:32.690
as well as typical controls.

00:40:32.690 --> 00:40:35.270
Since then, we replicated the study,

00:40:35.270 --> 00:40:37.210
other people replicated the study.

00:40:37.210 --> 00:40:40.640
We also extended the
study, trying to understand

00:40:40.640 --> 00:40:43.630
what are the factors that are separating

00:40:43.630 --> 00:40:45.500
child with autism from typically

00:40:45.500 --> 00:40:47.670
or developmentally-late children.

00:40:47.670 --> 00:40:50.090
And we found that the
factors that are particularly

00:40:50.090 --> 00:40:52.580
important are the factors that have to do

00:40:52.580 --> 00:40:55.420
with presence of speech
or combined presence

00:40:55.420 --> 00:40:57.143
of speech and eye contact.

00:40:59.510 --> 00:41:03.160
What we also did is we
ask a related question.

00:41:03.160 --> 00:41:06.520
We know that sensitivity
to faces and speech

00:41:06.520 --> 00:41:08.950
is present already in newborns.

00:41:08.950 --> 00:41:11.810
When we show a newborn
a face-like stimulus,

00:41:11.810 --> 00:41:15.890
or we play human speech, they are capable

00:41:15.890 --> 00:41:19.255
of orienting preferentially
toward these stimuli.

00:41:19.255 --> 00:41:22.248
It's a very elementary
orienting mechanism,

00:41:22.248 --> 00:41:26.420
subcortically mediated,
something that gives a newborn

00:41:26.420 --> 00:41:29.230
sort of a leg up to know how to,

00:41:29.230 --> 00:41:31.733
where to find the most important things

00:41:31.733 --> 00:41:35.830
that are going to be
relevant to their survival.

00:41:35.830 --> 00:41:38.370
So what we did is we asked
the following question,

00:41:38.370 --> 00:41:41.330
we see these deficits
in response to speech

00:41:41.330 --> 00:41:44.400
and eye contact at 18 months.

00:41:44.400 --> 00:41:48.570
Can we see them before
behavioral symptoms come online?

00:41:48.570 --> 00:41:51.730
And we conducted a study
in younger siblings

00:41:51.730 --> 00:41:54.120
of children with autism,
and as we discussed,

00:41:54.120 --> 00:41:57.023
about 20% of them are
likely to have autism.

00:41:58.160 --> 00:42:01.520
Another 30, 40 maybe,
broader autism phenotype.

00:42:01.520 --> 00:42:04.960
And the remaining children
will be developing typically.

00:42:04.960 --> 00:42:07.850
And we show them the same kind of task

00:42:07.850 --> 00:42:12.660
as we did to children
who already have autism.

00:42:12.660 --> 00:42:15.740
And as you can see, this blue bar is lower

00:42:16.630 --> 00:42:19.940
than even that of the children
with broader autism phenotype

00:42:19.940 --> 00:42:22.410
which suggests that,
already at six months,

00:42:22.410 --> 00:42:27.290
babies who are later become
symptomatic of autism,

00:42:27.290 --> 00:42:30.610
they already are showing
selective difficulty

00:42:30.610 --> 00:42:33.700
in attending to faces, especially faces

00:42:33.700 --> 00:42:37.230
that try to engage them in
a face-to-face interaction.

00:42:37.230 --> 00:42:40.752
Since then, we replicated
it with a different set

00:42:40.752 --> 00:42:42.690
of stimuli, and more recently,

00:42:42.690 --> 00:42:44.830
we actually replicated this phenomenon

00:42:47.211 --> 00:42:49.660
on a context of a
face-to-face interaction.

00:42:49.660 --> 00:42:53.030
We took a very, very precise
and very detailed measurement

00:42:53.030 --> 00:42:55.570
of the child's attention as
the child was interacting

00:42:55.570 --> 00:42:56.483
with the person.

00:42:57.832 --> 00:43:01.070
And what we found was one condition

00:43:01.070 --> 00:43:03.130
in which children who
later developed autism

00:43:03.130 --> 00:43:04.910
differed from those who didn't,

00:43:04.910 --> 00:43:07.735
and this was the condition when the person

00:43:07.735 --> 00:43:10.130
sat in front of the child and spoke.

00:43:10.130 --> 00:43:13.070
Not a condition when she
sang or played peekaboo.

00:43:13.070 --> 00:43:15.290
It's only when she spoke,

00:43:15.290 --> 00:43:17.360
which was quite remarkable
because it really shows

00:43:17.360 --> 00:43:19.830
the correspondence of
what we see in real world

00:43:19.830 --> 00:43:23.913
and what we see in the
laboratory conditions.

00:43:25.310 --> 00:43:28.603
So what we do from here?

00:43:30.590 --> 00:43:34.490
We are beginning to see
signals as early as six months

00:43:34.490 --> 00:43:36.453
in babies who later develop autism.

00:43:37.830 --> 00:43:41.190
These attentional difficulties
are likely to be driven

00:43:41.190 --> 00:43:44.900
by atypical development of brain networks

00:43:44.900 --> 00:43:49.700
that are involved in prioritizing
stimuli for processing,

00:43:49.700 --> 00:43:53.113
selecting certain visual/auditory
inputs over others.

00:43:54.980 --> 00:43:57.800
These systems develop very early.

00:43:57.800 --> 00:44:01.980
They develop as early as during pregnancy.

00:44:01.980 --> 00:44:05.320
So what we are doing now,
we are actually extending

00:44:05.320 --> 00:44:10.240
downward our studies, and focus on late,

00:44:10.240 --> 00:44:13.073
prenatal and early postnatal development.

00:44:14.170 --> 00:44:16.530
What I mean by that is that we are looking

00:44:16.530 --> 00:44:21.110
for the roots of social and
attentional difficulties

00:44:21.110 --> 00:44:23.290
that we see at six months

00:44:23.290 --> 00:44:26.240
already during the last
trimester of pregnancy

00:44:26.240 --> 00:44:28.250
and shortly after birth.

00:44:28.250 --> 00:44:29.930
So how do we do that?

00:44:29.930 --> 00:44:34.370
We do that by conducting
brain imaging studies.

00:44:34.370 --> 00:44:37.910
And what we are using is the resting state

00:44:37.910 --> 00:44:40.850
functional connectivity approach.

00:44:40.850 --> 00:44:43.063
This is a very non-invasive,

00:44:44.020 --> 00:44:47.340
non-demanding and safe approach

00:44:47.340 --> 00:44:51.393
to learning about brain
function and brain development.

00:44:52.820 --> 00:44:56.269
What happens is that in a brain,

00:44:56.269 --> 00:45:00.380
resting state partners
arise spontaneously,

00:45:00.380 --> 00:45:03.000
so we can image a child who's asleep

00:45:03.000 --> 00:45:07.150
and still draw conclusions
about how the brain

00:45:07.150 --> 00:45:11.430
is wired to process specific
kinds of information.

00:45:11.430 --> 00:45:14.620
We can evaluate networks
that are dedicated

00:45:14.620 --> 00:45:18.660
to processing visual or
auditory information.

00:45:18.660 --> 00:45:22.370
We can evaluate how the
networks that are involved

00:45:22.370 --> 00:45:25.730
in selection of stimuli
for processing function

00:45:25.730 --> 00:45:27.540
very, very early on.

00:45:27.540 --> 00:45:31.220
The reason why this approach
is likely to be effective,

00:45:31.220 --> 00:45:34.040
that we know that in older
individuals with autism,

00:45:34.040 --> 00:45:36.510
some of the networks that I just mentioned

00:45:36.510 --> 00:45:38.943
are functioning atypically.

00:45:40.710 --> 00:45:43.423
So what do we know about current,

00:45:44.710 --> 00:45:46.170
what do we know about current

00:45:48.380 --> 00:45:52.200
state of network development during

00:45:53.034 --> 00:45:54.640
the last trimester of pregnancy?

00:45:54.640 --> 00:45:57.350
It used to be that researchers thought

00:45:57.350 --> 00:46:01.780
that during the last
three months of pregnancy,

00:46:01.780 --> 00:46:04.620
what we can see emerging
in the developing brain

00:46:04.620 --> 00:46:06.690
are largely sensory networks.

00:46:06.690 --> 00:46:10.543
These are networks that
are involved in motor,

00:46:12.895 --> 00:46:15.730
in processing visual
stimuli, auditory stimuli,

00:46:15.730 --> 00:46:18.570
or, for instance, motor function.

00:46:18.570 --> 00:46:21.710
More recent studies suggest that even

00:46:21.710 --> 00:46:25.300
before the babies are born,
they are certain foundations

00:46:25.300 --> 00:46:28.810
that are being laid for
development of brain areas

00:46:28.810 --> 00:46:31.690
that involved in emotion processing,

00:46:31.690 --> 00:46:36.370
in processing of speech as well
as social stimuli as faces.

00:46:36.370 --> 00:46:39.340
After the babies are born
in the neo-natal brains,

00:46:39.340 --> 00:46:42.283
we are beginning to see the networks

00:46:42.283 --> 00:46:45.320
that are very important for
us such as saliency network,

00:46:45.320 --> 00:46:48.840
executive control network,
or default mode networks

00:46:48.840 --> 00:46:51.883
already form and emerge
and become functional.

00:46:52.780 --> 00:46:56.490
And we also know that the
properties of the networks

00:46:56.490 --> 00:46:59.940
are actually linked
with later development.

00:46:59.940 --> 00:47:02.880
They are linked with language,

00:47:02.880 --> 00:47:05.270
cognition and emotional development.

00:47:05.270 --> 00:47:06.810
So what we are targeting right now

00:47:06.810 --> 00:47:11.157
is something that may actually tell us

00:47:11.157 --> 00:47:14.560
something about the
mechanisms that underlie

00:47:14.560 --> 00:47:17.133
early symptoms of autism.

00:47:18.220 --> 00:47:21.410
And we are tremendously
excited about this work

00:47:21.410 --> 00:47:24.520
because this is truly
a groundbreaking work,

00:47:24.520 --> 00:47:27.000
and we are sort of at the forefront

00:47:27.000 --> 00:47:30.290
of the new research we started.

00:47:30.290 --> 00:47:32.543
We already started enrolling families.

00:47:33.380 --> 00:47:36.520
We have over 20 families
already following,

00:47:36.520 --> 00:47:38.448
which is fantastic.

00:47:38.448 --> 00:47:40.280
If you hear of any family
interested in participating

00:47:40.280 --> 00:47:42.643
in our studies, please spread the word.

00:47:46.140 --> 00:47:50.220
Everything that we do in our work,

00:47:50.220 --> 00:47:52.650
everything that we do in our clinics,

00:47:52.650 --> 00:47:54.820
everything that we do in our labs

00:47:56.180 --> 00:47:58.763
is really designed toward one purpose.

00:48:00.524 --> 00:48:01.840
And this is sort of my favorite video,

00:48:01.840 --> 00:48:06.470
and the purpose is to make sure

00:48:06.470 --> 00:48:10.260
that every child can live
up to their potential

00:48:10.260 --> 00:48:13.040
at their best, and bring as much joy

00:48:13.040 --> 00:48:15.713
as we can to their lives.

00:48:22.944 --> 00:48:25.440
(audience awes)

00:48:25.440 --> 00:48:29.190
Whoops, so I wanted to thank people,

00:48:29.190 --> 00:48:33.010
without whom none of the work I talked

00:48:33.010 --> 00:48:34.650
about would be possible here.

00:48:34.650 --> 00:48:38.160
We have a clinical team,
we are running a clinic

00:48:38.160 --> 00:48:40.290
for infants and toddlers who are at risk

00:48:40.290 --> 00:48:42.020
for developmental disorders,

00:48:42.020 --> 00:48:44.563
or exhibiting symptoms of
developmental disorders.

00:48:45.802 --> 00:48:48.620
It's the Yale Toddler
Developmental Disabilities Clinic.

00:48:48.620 --> 00:48:51.490
And we also have a research lab called

00:48:51.490 --> 00:48:54.970
Social and Affective
Neuroscience of Autism Program.

00:48:54.970 --> 00:48:57.653
Our group is very interdisciplinary,

00:48:58.720 --> 00:49:00.840
brings experts in multiple areas.

00:49:00.840 --> 00:49:04.941
And these are people
who are the most amazing

00:49:04.941 --> 00:49:07.186
people to work with, and I'm truly honored

00:49:07.186 --> 00:49:09.570
to be able to do that for
so many years here at Yale.

00:49:09.570 --> 00:49:12.560
So big shoutout to the entire team.

00:49:12.560 --> 00:49:15.727
(audience applauding)

00:49:17.020 --> 00:49:21.340
And thanks to the
families who really bring,

00:49:21.340 --> 00:49:23.490
make everything that we do possible.

00:49:23.490 --> 00:49:27.319
And our funders who have
to be mentioned as well,

00:49:27.319 --> 00:49:30.766
they are tremendously important,
influential in our life.

00:49:30.766 --> 00:49:32.925
So thank you so much for your attention,

00:49:32.925 --> 00:49:35.513
and I'll be happy to
take some questions now.

00:49:38.201 --> 00:49:41.618
(serene twinkling music)

